  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nFollowing on from https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12151 and in regards to the way the [HTML AAM discussion](https://github.com/w3c/html-aam/issues/601) is going, if someone sets `alt=\"\"` (marking the image as decorative) and `controls` on an image, the `alt=\"\"` will be ignored so that at least someone using a screen reader will be aware that there is an unnamed image present.   But, I'd imagine that might not be expected for a developer who has always used `alt=\"\"` to hide the image from the accessibility tree.\n\nShouldn't there be at least a note in the spec for `controls`, or in the section about the [alt attribute](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#attr-img-alt)) to indicate that if someone is going to add the controls attribute to an image, that it also needs alternative text so someone using assistive technology knows what controls are in reference to - aside from just an \"image\"?\n\n\n\n",
